esign: My approach is to use the YOLOv8 segmentation model, which is well-suited for instance segmentation tasks. I will first convert the provided `train_dataset` and `val_dataset` into the YOLO format using the built-in `generate_yolo_dataset` method. Then, I will train a `yolov8s-seg` model on this data for 50 epochs with an image size of 1024 to avoid losing detail from downsampling. After training, I will load the best-performing model weights and perform inference on each image in the validation set. Finally, I will process the model\'s output masks, assigning a unique integer ID to each detected cell instance and combining them into a single `uint16` mask per image as required.\nResults: The YOLOv8n-seg model was trained for only two epochs, which proved insufficient for the model to learn the cell segmentation task effectively. While the training and prediction pipeline executed without errors, the resulting validation metrics (mAP50-95 of ~0.04) and the final SEG score of 0.0027 are extremely low. This indicates that the model is underfitted and did not have enough time to converge on a meaningful solution.\nValidation Metric: 0.002701166303111972\nAnalysis: The YOLOv8n-seg model was trained for only two epochs, which proved insufficient for the model to learn the cell segmentation task effectively. While the training and prediction pipeline executed without errors, the resulting validation metrics (mAP50-95 of ~0.04) and the final SEG score of 0.0027 are extremely low. This indicates that the model is underfitted and did not have enough time to converge on a meaningful solution.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: My strategy is to address the previous model\'s underfitting by using a more capable model and a longer training schedule. I will employ the `YOLOv8s-seg` model, a significant step up from the nano version, and train it for 50 epochs, which should be sufficient for convergence on this task without exceeding the time limit. The image size 