sfully. To accommodate the larger model\'s memory requirements, I will also reduce the batch size from 4 to 2 and correct a critical bug in the post-processing logic to ensure that higher-confidence masks correctly take precedence in overlapping regions.\nResults: The YOLOv8n-seg model was trained for 2 epochs and the script executed successfully. However, the model\'s performance is extremely poor, achieving a final SEG score of only 0.0027. The validation logs show a very low recall of approximately 0.05, indicating that the model is failing to detect the vast majority of cell instances.\nValidation Metric: 0.002701166303111972\nAnalysis: The YOLOv8n-seg model was trained for 2 epochs and the script executed successfully. However, the model\'s performance is extremely poor, achieving a final SEG score of only 0.0027. The validation logs show a very low recall of approximately 0.05, indicating that the model is failing to detect the vast majority of cell instances.\n\n-------------------------------\nDebug plan: The previous solution timed out due to an excessively long training schedule with a medium-sized model, which was too computationally demanding for the given time limit. To resolve this, I will significantly reduce the computational load to ensure the program completes. I will switch from the `yolov8m-seg` model back to the smaller and faster `yolov8s-seg` model to accelerate training and inference. Furthermore, I will drastically cut the number of training epochs from 25 to 10 and reduce the early stopping patience to 5, which will substantially decrease the total runtime while retaining the high-resolution processing necessary for biological images.\nResults: The YOLOv8n-seg model was trained for two epochs, but the resulting performance was very poor, achieving a final SEG score of approximately 0.0027. The training logs indicate that while precision was high, recall was extremely low (~0.05). This suggests the model failed to detect the vast majority of